{"id": "2026-09-16-news-kosovo-tribunal-convicts-tha-i-of-war-crimes", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Kosovo tribunal convicts Thaçi of war crimes", "summary": "The Kosovo Specialist Chambers found ex-president Hashim Thaçi and three co-defendants guilty of war crimes and sentenced Thaçi to 25 years.", "url": "https://www.scp-ks.org/en/hashim-thaci-and-co-defendants-found-guilty-war-crimes", "source_domain": "scp-ks.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The Kosovo Specialist Chambers convicted former Kosovo president Hashim Thaçi and three co-defendants of war crimes on Wednesday 16 September 2026, sentencing Thaçi to 25 years in prison. The Hague-based court — an EU-backed tribunal staffed by international judges but applying Kosovo law, set up to try crimes committed during and after the 1998–99 independence war — found Thaçi, Jakup Krasniqi (also 25 years), Kadri Veseli (18) and Rexhep Selimi (13) criminally responsible for arbitrary detention of 385 people, cruel treatment of 49, torture of 303 and the murder of 96, committed as leaders of the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA). The panel acquitted all four of crimes against humanity, ruling prosecutors had not proved beyond reasonable doubt a \"widespread or systematic attack\" on civilians, and cleared them on a number of specific incidents. Thaçi, who resigned the presidency in 2020 to face the indictment, has been in the tribunal's detention since.", "why": "it is the first conviction of the KLA's wartime top command, and the sharpest legal reckoning yet with abuses on the victors' side of a war whose victims were largely fellow Kosovo Albanians branded as collaborators.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-kosovo-tribunal-convicts-tha-i-of-war-crimes", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Hashim Thaçi", "Kosovo", "Kosovo Specialist Chambers", "KLA"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-house-passes-iran-war-powers-measure", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "House passes Iran war-powers measure", "summary": "The US House voted 220–204 to bar further US military action against Iran without congressional approval, its third such vote.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/16/us-house-votes-to-end-iran-war-7-republicans-join-in-favour", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The US House of Representatives voted 220–204 late on Tuesday 15 September 2026 to invoke the War Powers Resolution and bar further US military action against Iran without congressional authorisation. The War Powers Resolution is the 1973 law that lets Congress force an end to hostilities the president began without a declaration of war. This was the House's third such vote since the summer; the earlier two did not halt the campaign, and this one still needs the Senate and would face a presidential veto, so its immediate legal effect is limited. Seven Republicans joined all Democrats, three of them backing the measure for the first time. The vote lands as a leaked Pentagon assessment, reported by Swiss broadcaster SRF, warns the Iran campaign has drawn down US munitions stockpiles to dangerous levels.", "why": "a growing cross-party bloc in Congress is on record against an open-ended war, even if it cannot yet compel the administration to stop.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-house-passes-iran-war-powers-measure", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["US Congress", "Iran", "Donald Trump", "War Powers Resolution"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-saudi-coalition-downs-drone-near-mecca", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Saudi coalition downs drone near Mecca", "summary": "Saudi Arabia said its air defences intercepted a Houthi drone near Mecca and warned that any attack on the city was a red line.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/saudi-arabia-says-houthis-target-mecca-warns-of-red-line/a-79285099", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Saudi Arabia said on Wednesday 16 September 2026 that its air defences intercepted a drone launched by Yemen's Houthis near Mecca, and warned that any attack on Islam's holiest city was a \"red line\". The Saudi-led coalition said the drone was destroyed just outside restricted airspace over the city, which draws millions of pilgrims for the annual hajj. The Iran-aligned Houthis denied targeting Mecca. The claim, which independent sources have not verified, marks a further widening of the Saudi–Houthi front within the broader Middle East conflict.", "why": "an attack on Mecca — real or merely alleged — is the kind of symbolic escalation that could pull Riyadh back into direct fighting in Yemen after years of an uneasy truce.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-saudi-coalition-downs-drone-near-mecca", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Saudi Arabia", "Houthis", "Mecca", "Yemen"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-seoul-court-fines-north-korea-for-2020-blast", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Seoul court fines North Korea for 2020 blast", "summary": "A Seoul court ordered North Korea to pay about $32.5m for demolishing the inter-Korean liaison office in 2020.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/16/seoul-court-orders-north-korea-to-pay-32-5m-for-blowing-up-office", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A court in Seoul on Wednesday 16 September 2026 ordered North Korea to pay South Korea's government 44.6 billion won (about $32.5 million) for demolishing the Inter-Korean Liaison Office in 2020. The Seoul Central District Court ruled Pyongyang liable for blowing up the building — a joint contact office in the Kaesong Industrial Complex meant to ease relations — which the North destroyed in June 2020 amid a row over anti-Pyongyang leaflets floated across the border. It is the first time Seoul has sought damages directly from the North Korean state. Analysts consider it all but certain Pyongyang will never pay; Seoul said it would \"review the necessary follow-up measures\".", "why": "with no realistic enforcement, the ruling is mostly symbolic, but it formalises a legal claim South Korea could press later and underscores how far inter-Korean ties have collapsed.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-seoul-court-fines-north-korea-for-2020-blast", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["South Korea", "North Korea", "Seoul Central District Court"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-neutrality-initiative-headed-for-defeat", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Neutrality initiative headed for defeat", "summary": "A second SRG poll found 63% of voters intend to reject the SVP-backed neutrality initiative ahead of the 27 September vote.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/2-srg-umfrage-klarer-nein-trend-bei-der-neutralitaetsinitiative", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A second SRG poll published Wednesday 16 September 2026 found 63% of voters intend to reject the neutrality initiative and 34% to back it, ahead of the federal vote on Sunday 27 September 2026. Opposition has hardened by nine points since the first poll (54% against), and 83% of respondents now report a firm voting intention, pollster GFS Bern said. The SVP-backed initiative would write a strict, \"comprehensive\" neutrality into the constitution — barring Switzerland from joining military alliances or adopting non-UN sanctions, such as the EU measures Bern took against Russia. The same poll showed the separate food-supply initiative on the 27 September ballot also losing support.", "why": "a clear No would settle, at least for now, whether Switzerland can keep aligning with EU sanctions without breaching its neutrality — a live question for Bern's foreign policy.", "thread_id": "2026-08-22-weekend-neutrality-initiative-trails-in-first-swiss-poll", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-22", "event_date": "2026-09-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "SVP", "GFS Bern"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-revolut-seeks-swiss-banking-licence", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Revolut seeks Swiss banking licence", "summary": "UK neobank Revolut has applied to FINMA for a Swiss banking licence, pledging over 150 million francs of investment.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/wirtschaft/finma-prueft-antrag-revolut-beantragt-banklizenz-in-der-schweiz", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "British neobank Revolut has applied to Switzerland's financial regulator FINMA for a Swiss banking licence, the broadcaster SRF reported on Wednesday 16 September 2026. A neobank is a bank that operates entirely through an app, with no branches. Revolut — already used by more than a million people in Switzerland under its EU licence — says it will invest over 150 million francs in the Swiss market over five years; a Swiss licence would let it offer domestic accounts, Swiss IBANs and deposit protection, competing head-on with incumbents. FINMA has not commented on the pending application.", "why": "a licensed Revolut would be the most direct challenge yet to Switzerland's retail banks from a low-cost digital entrant.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-revolut-seeks-swiss-banking-licence", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Revolut", "FINMA", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-vaud-unveils-2027-budget-356m-deficit", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Vaud unveils 2027 budget, 356M deficit", "summary": "Vaud's cantonal government presented a draft 2027 budget with a 356-million-franc deficit within the constitutional balance rule.", "url": "https://www.vd.ch/actualites/communiques-de-presse-de-letat-de-vaud/detail/communique/le-conseil-detat-presente-un-projet-de-budget-2027-rigoureux-affichant-une-croissance-contenue-des-charges", "source_domain": "vd.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Vaud cantonal government presented its draft 2027 budget on 14 September 2026, projecting a 356-million-franc deficit that stays within the constitutional \"petit équilibre\" balanced-budget rule. The cantonal executive (Conseil d'État) put projected revenues at 12.859 billion francs, up 3.4% (417 million) on 2026, and added 317 net new full-time posts. Officials framed the plan as \"rigorous\" cost discipline that still respects the spending limits fixed in the cantonal constitution.", "why": "the numbers set the terms for the autumn budget fight in the Grand Conseil and signal how much room Vaud has for new spending on schools, health and staffing next year.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-vaud-unveils-2027-budget-356m-deficit", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Vaud", "Conseil d'État"]}
{"id": "2026-09-16-news-swiss-rail-accessibility-still-lags-for-disabled", "date": "2026-09-16", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss rail accessibility still lags for disabled", "summary": "Le Temps reports wheelchair users still face gaps in Swiss public-transport accessibility despite years of upgrades.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/il-y-a-des-ameliorations-mais-en-suisse-les-personnes-en-situation-de-handicap-galerent-encore-dans-les-transports-publics", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Wheelchair users still face gaps in Swiss public-transport accessibility despite years of upgrades, Le Temps reported on 16 September 2026. The paper followed passengers who depend on pre-booked boarding help from the railways: one traveller at Renens station (Vaud) missed his train to Lausanne when the CFF assistance agent booked the day before failed to appear. Riders describe improvements over the past decade alongside persistent problems — poorly marked wheelchair spaces, occupied reserved seats, and lapses in the assistance service. The account sits against Switzerland's legal deadline, under the 2004 disability-equality act, for barrier-free public transport, which much of the network has missed.", "why": "it is a concrete measure of how far \"accessible\" transport still falls short of the law for the people who rely on it.", "thread_id": "2026-09-16-news-swiss-rail-accessibility-still-lags-for-disabled", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-16", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["CFF", "Switzerland", "Vaud"]}
